00:28:38,420 --> 00:28:40,900
So similarly, if you've already gone and paid the training costs,

212
00:28:41,220 --> 00:28:43,660
you're not going to go and fire the person because you wish

213
00:28:43,660 --> 00:28:46,260
that you hadn't wasted, spent the training costs.

214
00:28:46,620 --> 00:28:50,660
You'll only go and replace them if the improvement,

215
00:28:51,020 --> 00:28:53,180
in addition to paying the training costs a second time,

216
00:28:53,180 --> 00:28:56,540
justifies getting rid of the person, which is fairly straight.

217
00:28:57,140 --> 00:28:58,340
Right? And then, of course, on top of it,

218
00:28:58,340 --> 00:29:01,660
there's also the name the puppy phenomenon, where once you get

219
00:29:01,660 --> 00:29:03,220
to know the person, you feel sorry for them

220
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:06,740
when you fire them, which might be that the boss feels sorry,

221
00:29:06,740 --> 00:29:08,420
might be the other workers feel sorry.

222
00:29:08,980 --> 00:29:11,380
There is the great George Clooney movie, Up in the Air,

223
00:29:11,380 --> 00:29:13,420
where he's a professional firing consultant.

224
00:29:14,260 --> 00:29:15,460
And what does he do?

225
00:29:15,460 --> 00:29:17,740
He fires people so the boss doesn't have to fire them.

226
00:29:18,660 --> 00:29:21,460
Now, anyone can fire a person.

227
00:29:21,460 --> 00:29:26,580
It's just opening your mouth and saying you are fired.

228
00:29:27,260 --> 00:29:29,580
But it's a very unpleasant thing to do.

229
00:29:29,580 --> 00:29:32,540
And so in the movie, George Clooney actually is paid to fly

230
00:29:32,540 --> 00:29:34,020
in around the country and do it.

231
00:29:34,540 --> 00:29:35,420
I did some research.

232
00:29:35,420 --> 00:29:38,060
So there is not, there isn't literally anyone

233
00:29:38,060 --> 00:29:39,940
that has been found who has George Clooney's job.

234
00:29:40,860 --> 00:29:43,740
So there's no such thing as someone, as far as anyone can find,

235
00:29:44,060 --> 00:29:45,420
who only fires people.

236
00:29:46,180 --> 00:29:48,940
There's a more general occupation where you do

237
00:29:48,940 --> 00:29:50,180
that plus other things.

238
00:29:50,180 --> 00:29:53,620
But it's not, but there's no one who just fires people all day,

239
00:29:53,620 --> 00:29:55,500
every day, and they fly around the world firing people.

240
00:29:56,420 --> 00:29:59,660
Yes. You know, they're consultants, and they may be

241
00:29:59,660 --> 00:30:01,180
like human capital consultants or whatever.

242
00:30:01,180 --> 00:30:02,940
And one of the things they do is help you fire people.

243
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:05,420
But they would also give you other advice, too.

244
00:30:06,060 --> 00:30:08,060
Yes, my wife was once at a law firm

245
00:30:08,060 --> 00:30:10,580
where there was one partner who brought in much less money

246
00:30:10,580 --> 00:30:11,420
than anybody else.

247
00:30:12,100 --> 00:30:14,220
And a condition of him remaining a partner was that he had

248
00:30:14,220 --> 00:30:16,380
to do all the unpleasant things that the other partners didn't

249
00:30:16,380 --> 00:30:17,820
want to, including firing people.

250
00:30:17,820 --> 00:30:21,140
So he was the guy who had to personally fire people,

251
00:30:21,500 --> 00:30:25,580
which was considered enough that they would keep treating him

252
00:30:25,580 --> 00:30:27,780
because he got to be a partner even though he wasn't bringing

253
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:29,140
in nearly as much money as other people.

254
00:30:29,140 --> 00:30:32,300
So, well, he might have been allowed to earn the money

255
00:30:32,300 --> 00:30:34,220
that someone who was bringing in a million dollars

256
00:30:34,220 --> 00:30:35,380
for a business would earn.

257
00:30:35,660 --> 00:30:37,660
So he was, so no one would have to feel mean.

258
00:30:37,660 --> 00:30:38,460
All right.

259
00:30:38,460 --> 00:30:42,620
So that's another case where it starts off,

260
00:30:42,620 --> 00:30:44,340
before you actually hire a person,

261
00:30:44,340 --> 00:30:46,420
where there's so many different people you might hire,

262
00:30:46,420 --> 00:30:50,180
but once the market proceeds, then the universe shrinks.

263
00:30:50,660 --> 00:30:53,260
And then there is, again, a lot of room for strategy.

264
00:30:53,940 --> 00:30:57,260
And, of course, also room for psychology, as this is suggesting.

265
00:30:57,260 --> 00:31:00,020
Whereas in general equilibrium,

266
00:31:00,020 --> 00:31:01,540
there's not much room for psychology.

267
00:31:01,540 --> 00:31:03,020
Again, you don't have to sit around worrying.

268
00:31:03,300 --> 00:31:04,860
But how will they feel if I do this?

269
00:31:05,580 --> 00:31:07,980
Like, how will the corn workers feel if they know

270
00:31:07,980 --> 00:31:10,100
that I didn't buy this can of corn for fear

271
00:31:10,100 --> 00:31:10,820
of raising the price?

272
00:31:10,820 --> 00:31:11,580
Will they resent me?

273
00:31:12,180 --> 00:31:15,220
Right? In general equilibrium, they have no idea who you are

274
00:31:15,220 --> 00:31:17,260
or what you've done, so they can't resent you.

275
00:31:17,940 --> 00:31:20,580
They can resent the general nature of customers.

276
00:31:21,340 --> 00:31:23,420
Right? So if you remember the movie Clerks,

277
00:31:23,420 --> 00:31:24,780
where the video clerk says,

278
00:31:24,780 --> 00:31:26,460
this job will be great if it works for the customers.

279
00:31:26,460 --> 00:31:28,260
So in general equilibrium,

280
00:31:28,260 --> 00:31:30,620
the clerks can resent customers in general,

281
00:31:31,020 --> 00:31:32,540
but they won't resent you personally.

282
00:31:33,100 --> 00:31:34,940
Because they don't know, they don't even,

283
00:31:34,940 --> 00:31:36,740
they don't even, aren't even aware you're alive.

284
00:31:37,020 --> 00:31:38,180
They can say, I don't like that job,

285
00:31:38,180 --> 00:31:39,540
because dealing with the public is bad.

286
00:31:39,540 --> 00:31:42,460
But you cannot personally convince someone

287
00:31:42,460 --> 00:31:44,100
that they want to quit by being in charge of them.

288
00:31:44,100 --> 00:31:45,260
In general equilibrium.

289
00:31:45,260 --> 00:31:45,780
All right.

290
00:31:46,260 --> 00:31:47,980
Anyway, so what game theory tries

291
00:31:47,980 --> 00:31:51,380
to do is analyze situations where strategy does matter,

292
00:31:51,380 --> 00:31:55,940
where we're somewhere in between the extreme poles of one man

293
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:59,260
versus nature, and one person versus,

294
00:31:59,260 --> 00:32:01,740
or an anonymous person versus the entirety of society.

295
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:05,020
Okay. All right.

296
00:32:05,380 --> 00:32:07,300
So, now game theory generally ends

297
00:32:07,300 --> 00:32:09,460
up with much less precise answers

298
00:32:09,460 --> 00:32:10,300
than general equilibrium.

299
00:32:10,780 --> 00:32:14,060
General equilibrium is noted for giving very precise answers

300
00:32:14,060 --> 00:32:20,380
but if you think the answers are completely wrong,

301
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:21,940
the precision is no longer so good.

302
00:32:22,740 --> 00:32:25,300
Right. There's a line that Richard Thaler repeats a lot.

303
00:32:25,300 --> 00:32:26,940
I don't know whether he started it.

304
00:32:26,940 --> 00:32:30,140
It's a line of I would rather be vaguely right than clearly wrong.

305
00:32:30,820 --> 00:32:33,180
So I'd rather write a book saying game theory can kind

306
00:32:33,180 --> 00:32:36,020
of illuminate a bunch of situations to some degree,

307
00:32:36,340 --> 00:32:39,060
which is true, rather than write Fisher Black's book saying

308
00:32:39,060 --> 00:32:41,100
general equilibrium theory predicts everything perfectly.

309
00:32:41,100 --> 00:32:43,860
And it's like, well, it predicts everything precisely,

310
00:32:44,540 --> 00:32:47,820
but the very precision lets us know that it's wrong, it seems.

311
00:32:49,820 --> 00:32:50,100
All right.

312
00:32:50,620 --> 00:32:53,140
So how many people here have done at least some game theory

313
00:32:53,140 --> 00:32:53,660
at some point?

314
00:32:53,820 --> 00:32:57,660
Okay. So a lot of this will be review, but I've known people

315
00:32:57,660 --> 00:33:00,220
to mess this up long after they've seen it a bunch of times.

316
00:33:00,220 --> 00:33:03,500
So let's go over the review again.

317
00:33:05,060 --> 00:33:06,620
Okay. All right.

318
00:33:06,620 --> 00:33:12,620
So the standard consumer theory still provides the building

319
00:33:12,620 --> 00:33:13,540
blocks for game theory.

320
00:33:14,180 --> 00:33:18,700
So we still have utility functions in game theory.

321
00:33:18,700 --> 00:33:20,660
So we still have utility functions.

322
00:33:21,300 --> 00:33:25,060
There is one change in the packaging,

323
00:33:25,460 --> 00:33:27,100
which is that in game theory people often talk

324
00:33:27,100 --> 00:33:30,620
about payoffs instead of utility.

325
00:33:30,780 --> 00:33:33,980
But payoffs is just another word for utility,

326
00:33:33,980 --> 00:33:35,780
so there's nothing new here.

327
00:33:35,780 --> 00:33:37,100
It's just other than a new word.

328
00:33:37,620 --> 00:33:37,780
All right.

329
00:33:37,780 --> 00:33:39,460
So the concept is the same.

330
00:33:39,460 --> 00:33:42,100
So just like with utility, the whole idea of utility is

331
00:33:42,100 --> 00:33:44,700
that x is a higher utility than y, and you have choices,

332
00:33:44,700 --> 00:33:45,740
but you can do either.

333
00:33:45,740 --> 00:33:47,340
You do the thing with higher utility.

334
00:33:47,660 --> 00:33:51,060
So payoffs, by definition, if something is a higher payoff

335
00:33:51,060 --> 00:33:52,780
than another thing, it means that you do the thing

336
00:33:52,780 --> 00:33:54,420
that is a higher payoff rather

337
00:33:54,420 --> 00:33:55,500
than the thing that is a lower payoff.

338
00:33:56,900 --> 00:33:58,100
Okay. All right.

339
00:33:58,580 --> 00:34:02,860
Now, there's just a little aside in case you know more about,

340
00:34:02,860 --> 00:34:06,100
you know, know something about expected utility theory.

341
00:34:06,100 --> 00:34:07,700
If you don't, don't worry about it.

342
00:34:07,700 --> 00:34:10,060
We'll talk about it in the lecture after the midterm.

343
00:34:10,580 --> 00:34:15,660
But just in case you were curious, in game theory,

344
00:34:15,660 --> 00:34:17,340
we usually use the term utilities

345
00:34:17,340 --> 00:34:20,740
as von Neumann-Morgenstern utilities.

346
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:23,020
Right. Von Neumann-Morgenstern.

347
00:34:23,380 --> 00:34:26,020
If you've seen Dr. Strangelove, the guy who played Dr.

348
00:34:26,020 --> 00:34:27,620
Strangelove is based on von Neumann.

349
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:28,620
All right.

350
00:34:28,740 --> 00:34:32,300
So in any case, I'm like a super genius German

351
00:34:32,300 --> 00:34:34,860
with possibly homicidal tendencies,

352
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:40,020
but a great strategist, but perhaps lacking in decency.

353
00:34:40,140 --> 00:34:45,140
But anyway, so there's a particular version

354
00:34:45,140 --> 00:34:48,740
of utility theory, which is called von Neumann-Morgenstern

355
00:34:48,740 --> 00:34:49,580
utility theory.

356
00:34:50,020 --> 00:34:54,300
And the utilities in game theory are generally interpreted

357
00:34:54,300 --> 00:34:58,740
as these special utilities in order to avoid all questions

358
00:34:58,740 --> 00:34:59,340
of risk aversion.

359
00:34:59,340 --> 00:35:03,500
If this completely confuses you or seems weird,

360
00:35:03,860 --> 00:35:04,700
don't worry about it.

361
00:35:05,220 --> 00:35:06,540
We will talk about it in week eight.

362
00:35:07,940 --> 00:35:10,460
But anyway, so at any point I'm lecturing, you're saying, wait,

363
00:35:10,460 --> 00:35:11,660
why is it you get to go

364
00:35:11,660 --> 00:35:14,340
and just have people maximize average utilities?

365
00:35:14,340 --> 00:35:15,820
Don't we need to worry about risk aversion?

366
00:35:16,220 --> 00:35:18,300
The short answer is no, this is actually okay,

367
00:35:18,660 --> 00:35:20,780
because we are making an assumption

368
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:23,540
that will be explained later about why,

369
00:35:23,540 --> 00:35:25,100
which allows us to do that.

370
00:35:25,380 --> 00:35:26,820
So anyway, don't worry about that.

371
00:35:26,820 --> 00:35:30,180
Okay, all right, now there's a game that's going

372
00:35:30,180 --> 00:35:32,220
to be represented in two distinct ways.

373
00:35:32,820 --> 00:35:37,900
These two ways are known as extensive form and normal form.

374
00:35:41,020 --> 00:35:44,220
So what extensive forms do is they display every possible

375
00:35:44,420 --> 00:35:45,820
course of game events turn by turn.

376
00:35:46,820 --> 00:35:48,980
All right, so when you're showing the extensive form,

377
00:35:49,700 --> 00:35:52,060
like this, extensive form,

378
00:35:52,060 --> 00:35:56,700
then you will show a initial decision,

379
00:35:56,700 --> 00:35:57,620
oh this is where we start,

380
00:35:57,620 --> 00:35:59,060
so that symbolizes our starting point.

381
00:35:59,500 --> 00:36:01,740
And then that actor might have three choices,

382
00:36:02,420 --> 00:36:04,020
and then another actor gets to act.

383
00:36:04,540 --> 00:36:07,940
And that might have, this might lead to two choices

384
00:36:08,460 --> 00:36:10,540
in this part of the tree, three choices here,

385
00:36:10,540 --> 00:36:12,100
maybe only one choice over here.

386
00:36:12,100 --> 00:36:14,820
And then that could go and branch out to other things.

387
00:36:14,820 --> 00:36:18,300
And it could go on, so on.

388
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:24,020
And then eventually it always terminates in some final result,

389
00:36:24,020 --> 00:36:25,540
and then we write down payoffs.

390
00:36:26,100 --> 00:36:29,620
And the way payoffs work is they are always ordered sequentially.

391
00:36:30,100 --> 00:36:33,740
So if this were a four-player game,

392
00:36:34,100 --> 00:36:37,060
this would mean that player one gets three, player two gets seven,

393
00:36:37,060 --> 00:36:40,700
player, wait, player one gets three, two gets seven,

394
00:36:40,700 --> 00:36:43,020
three gets ten, player four gets a payoff of 13.

395
00:36:43,940 --> 00:36:45,860
All right, so at the end of each node,

396
00:36:45,860 --> 00:36:49,180
there will generally be parentheses with a series of numbers in it.

397
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:53,220
And each number in order corresponds to the payoff that each player gets.

398
00:36:53,220 --> 00:36:54,860
So player one, player two, player three,

399
00:36:54,860 --> 00:36:56,940
player four, all the way up to player n.

400
00:36:58,780 --> 00:37:01,140
In practice, economists almost always look at games

401
00:37:01,140 --> 00:37:04,500
with only two players, which simplifies things a lot.

402
00:37:05,420 --> 00:37:07,500
Once in a while, I guess I've seen a three-player game.

403
00:37:07,500 --> 00:37:09,620
I'm not sure I've ever seen a four-player game.

404
00:37:11,180 --> 00:37:13,540
I guess I've seen like infinite number of player games.

405
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:17,100
So yeah, one, two, three, and then infinity is the other case that we do.

406
00:37:17,860 --> 00:37:21,060
All right, if you were applying game theory to a real-world problem,

407
00:37:21,060 --> 00:37:23,940
of course, you would want to think about it a bit more deeply than that.

408
00:37:24,380 --> 00:37:27,020
And say, well, I can't think of anything like, sure,

409
00:37:27,020 --> 00:37:28,820
there's way more than three players involved,

410
00:37:28,820 --> 00:37:31,340
but I've only worked out models of three players,

411
00:37:31,340 --> 00:37:34,220
so let's assume away all those other people.

412
00:37:37,100 --> 00:37:39,420
So by the way, so I'm going to mention this again,

413
00:37:39,420 --> 00:37:41,420
but now just seems like a good time.

414
00:37:41,420 --> 00:37:45,860
So when we get to the exams, there will be two kinds of questions.

415
00:37:46,300 --> 00:37:47,740
There will be one kind of question that says,

416
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:49,260
in a certain model, what happens?

417
00:37:50,660 --> 00:37:53,420
When a question is like that, do you need to discuss the outside world

418
00:37:53,420 --> 00:37:54,540
in order to answer the question?

419
00:37:54,980 --> 00:37:57,540
No. Should you discuss the outside world in order to answer the question?

420
00:37:57,540 --> 00:37:59,180
No. If it asks what happens in a model,

421
00:37:59,500 --> 00:38:02,900
then you just work through logically what happens in that model,

422
00:38:02,900 --> 00:38:03,900
and that's the end.

423
00:38:04,300 --> 00:38:06,660
And in general, you don't need to say anything else.

424
00:38:06,660 --> 00:38:10,380
If it doesn't say discuss whether the model is realistic, you don't do it.

425
00:38:10,380 --> 00:38:13,940
On the other hand, I also often have a question that says,

426
00:38:13,940 --> 00:38:16,860
in the real world, when you have a question like that,

427
00:38:16,860 --> 00:38:19,060
is it okay to just go and say, well, in general equilibrium,

428
00:38:19,060 --> 00:38:20,300
this is how it works, the end?

429
00:38:21,380 --> 00:38:23,420
No. Not unless you actually say,

430
00:38:23,420 --> 00:38:25,740
generally, it fits the real world in this particular case.

431
00:38:26,740 --> 00:38:28,060
So this is one of the ways,

432
00:38:28,060 --> 00:38:30,780
so whenever you hear about people learning to think like economists,

433
00:38:30,780 --> 00:38:32,940
one of the scary things to me is,

434
00:38:32,940 --> 00:38:35,060
in the process of learning to think like an economist,

435
00:38:35,060 --> 00:38:38,780
many students will actually stop talking about the world at all,

436
00:38:38,780 --> 00:38:40,820
and then even when you ask them about the real world,

437
00:38:40,820 --> 00:38:43,780
they'll say, well, here's a model that is somehow related,

438
00:38:43,780 --> 00:38:46,020
and I'm just going to tell you what happens to that model,

439
00:38:46,020 --> 00:38:48,420
and since what I say does happen in the model,

440
00:38:48,420 --> 00:38:49,620
I've answered the question,

441
00:38:49,620 --> 00:38:51,820
even though the question didn't ask about the model at all.

442
00:38:51,820 --> 00:38:54,780
So anyway, I will remind you about this when we do the exams,

443
00:38:54,780 --> 00:39:00,020
but I have been disturbed at this one bad side effect of learning economics,

444
00:39:00,020 --> 00:39:03,100
which is forgetting that it is ultimately supposed to be about

445
00:39:03,100 --> 00:39:04,500
understanding the real world.

446
00:39:04,500 --> 00:39:07,500
At least you're not supposed to lose your ability to think like an economist.

447
00:39:07,500 --> 00:39:10,220
You're not supposed to lose your ability to understand the real world.

448
00:39:10,220 --> 00:39:12,540
So you're supposed to enhance that ability,

449
00:39:12,540 --> 00:39:16,660
not to just change the subject into models.

450
00:39:16,940 --> 00:39:20,500
It's useful to understand the models to see whether they want to

451
00:39:20,500 --> 00:39:23,060
understand the logic to know whether they do fit the real world,

452
00:39:23,060 --> 00:39:26,860
but that's not the same thing as understanding the real world.

453
00:39:26,860 --> 00:39:28,460
Anyway, all right.

454
00:39:28,460 --> 00:39:31,020
So this is an extensive form here,

455
00:39:31,020 --> 00:39:34,900
and as I said, so they show every possible thing that might logically happen,

456
00:39:34,900 --> 00:39:40,420
turn by turn, and we see how things branch out from every possibility.

457
00:39:40,420 --> 00:39:43,860
For this reason, these are often also called decision trees.

458
00:39:43,860 --> 00:39:45,860
Notice it's like an upside down tree.

459
00:39:45,860 --> 00:39:47,260
So really to look like a tree,

460
00:39:47,260 --> 00:39:50,380
we flip it around so that would be the roots of the tree,

461
00:39:50,380 --> 00:39:53,780
and then the trees will only branch out that way.

462
00:39:53,780 --> 00:39:56,540
You know what trees look like.

463
00:39:56,540 --> 00:39:58,900
There are some weird trees that aren't like that.

