00:42:21,880 --> 00:42:22,800
Popeye.

55
00:42:22,800 --> 00:42:23,920
All right.

56
00:42:23,920 --> 00:42:27,560
Anyway, so there's a character, a policeman named Popeye.

57
00:42:27,560 --> 00:42:29,600
And he's hunting a criminal in New York.

58
00:42:29,600 --> 00:42:32,400
And the criminal runs onto a subway.

59
00:42:32,400 --> 00:42:34,360
And then Popeye gets onto the subway.

60
00:42:34,360 --> 00:42:36,200
But then the criminal gets off the subway.

61
00:42:36,200 --> 00:42:37,200
Then Popeye gets off.

62
00:42:37,200 --> 00:42:39,320
And then they just sit there looking at each other.

63
00:42:39,320 --> 00:42:41,800
Because Popeye wants to be on the subway with the criminal

64
00:42:41,800 --> 00:42:43,520
to find out what's going on.

65
00:42:43,520 --> 00:42:46,560
And the criminal does not want to be on the same subway,

66
00:42:46,560 --> 00:42:48,560
be on the same place as Popeye.

67
00:42:48,560 --> 00:42:50,840
Because the criminal does not want the cop

68
00:42:50,840 --> 00:42:53,480
to know what is going on.

69
00:42:53,480 --> 00:42:56,040
So you can think about it as an extensive form

70
00:42:56,040 --> 00:42:58,560
where we start off with, let's see,

71
00:42:58,560 --> 00:43:03,120
with the criminal decides to be on or off of the subway.

72
00:43:03,120 --> 00:43:05,400
And then Popeye decides to be on or off.

73
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:12,920
And then when we're thinking about the payoffs,

74
00:43:12,920 --> 00:43:14,760
so the criminal is the first actor.

75
00:43:14,760 --> 00:43:17,080
So the criminal is their number one.

76
00:43:17,080 --> 00:43:20,160
And the cop, Popeye, is number two.

77
00:43:20,160 --> 00:43:25,080
So if they were both on, then which person

78
00:43:25,080 --> 00:43:27,800
is going to be getting the low payoff?

79
00:43:27,800 --> 00:43:28,400
The criminal.

80
00:43:28,400 --> 00:43:29,760
Yeah, the criminal's getting the low payoff.

81
00:43:29,760 --> 00:43:30,640
Let's call that zero.

82
00:43:30,640 --> 00:43:32,600
And the policeman's getting the good one of 10.

83
00:43:32,600 --> 00:43:34,240
He's happy.

84
00:43:34,240 --> 00:43:35,600
But then there's the other part.

85
00:43:35,600 --> 00:43:37,680
So if they're both on, on the other hand,

86
00:43:37,680 --> 00:43:41,600
if the criminal is on the subway and the cop is off the subway,

87
00:43:41,600 --> 00:43:45,000
then the criminal is happy and the cop is unhappy.

88
00:43:45,000 --> 00:43:47,440
If the criminal is off and the cop is on,

89
00:43:47,440 --> 00:43:49,040
then it's the same thing.

90
00:43:49,040 --> 00:43:51,040
So the criminal is happy and the cop is unhappy.

91
00:43:51,040 --> 00:43:52,720
And if they're both off, then it is

92
00:43:52,720 --> 00:43:56,960
the cop that is happy and the criminal that is unhappy.

93
00:43:56,960 --> 00:44:01,200
So we have all of those possibilities.

94
00:44:01,200 --> 00:44:02,700
Now, there's a bunch of complications

95
00:44:02,700 --> 00:44:05,640
about how you would deal with extensive forms.

96
00:44:05,640 --> 00:44:09,040
So one of them is, what about nature?

97
00:44:09,040 --> 00:44:11,080
What if there's also a possibility

98
00:44:11,080 --> 00:44:13,960
that lightning strikes the subway?

99
00:44:13,960 --> 00:44:15,920
And how does that change the game?

100
00:44:15,920 --> 00:44:18,880
And the way that you normally model nature

101
00:44:18,880 --> 00:44:23,040
is just put it in as a player that plays everything randomly.

102
00:44:23,040 --> 00:44:25,880
So sometimes you actually put nature in as a player.

103
00:44:25,880 --> 00:44:27,960
And then you'll say, well, either we're

104
00:44:27,960 --> 00:44:30,840
in state of the world one or state of the world two

105
00:44:30,840 --> 00:44:32,360
randomly.

106
00:44:32,360 --> 00:44:35,800
And so if nature is a player, why does it act randomly?

107
00:44:35,800 --> 00:44:37,300
Well, it would have to be that nature

108
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:39,000
has the same payoffs in every situation,

109
00:44:39,000 --> 00:44:41,240
so it's indifferent between all outcomes, which

110
00:44:41,240 --> 00:44:44,000
is just a philosophical claim.

111
00:44:44,000 --> 00:44:46,080
The universe doesn't care about you.

112
00:44:46,080 --> 00:44:48,960
It's either one way or the other.

113
00:44:48,960 --> 00:44:52,160
Now, there's also an issue with information sets.

114
00:44:52,160 --> 00:44:56,720
And that is, well, what about simultaneous moves?

115
00:44:56,720 --> 00:44:59,040
And the way that it is handled with extensive forms

116
00:44:59,040 --> 00:45:01,460
is to say, well, there's really no difference

117
00:45:01,460 --> 00:45:05,920
between moving simultaneously and moving sequentially

118
00:45:05,920 --> 00:45:09,440
without knowing what your opponent did.

119
00:45:09,440 --> 00:45:10,320
So think about this.

120
00:45:10,320 --> 00:45:12,960
Suppose we either play rock, paper, scissors sequentially,

121
00:45:12,960 --> 00:45:15,720
where we both play and we reveal at the same time.

122
00:45:15,720 --> 00:45:19,480
Or you could go and secretly write down

123
00:45:19,480 --> 00:45:21,040
what you want on your strategy.

124
00:45:21,040 --> 00:45:22,880
And then I go, and then I, without seeing

125
00:45:22,880 --> 00:45:24,800
what you wrote down, announce what I'm doing,

126
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:26,840
and then you open up the paper.

127
00:45:26,840 --> 00:45:29,920
For all practical purposes, those are the same thing.

128
00:45:29,920 --> 00:45:33,880
Either we play simultaneously or we play sequentially,

129
00:45:33,880 --> 00:45:36,320
but I don't know what you did.

130
00:45:36,320 --> 00:45:41,000
So the way that we handle this simultaneous play

131
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:43,600
with extensive forms is simply to say,

132
00:45:43,600 --> 00:45:46,840
that's just the same as making a decision without information.

133
00:45:46,840 --> 00:45:51,560
So then it's conventional to go and draw a dotted box

134
00:45:51,560 --> 00:45:57,040
around different places if you can't tell

135
00:45:57,040 --> 00:45:58,880
the difference between them.

136
00:45:58,880 --> 00:46:00,560
So if you don't know, did he do rock?

137
00:46:00,560 --> 00:46:01,320
Did he do paper?

138
00:46:01,320 --> 00:46:02,640
Or did he do scissors?

139
00:46:02,640 --> 00:46:05,080
If you don't know what he did, then rather than saying

140
00:46:05,080 --> 00:46:06,120
that you know what node you're at,

141
00:46:06,120 --> 00:46:08,400
we just draw a dotted line around all the nodes

142
00:46:08,400 --> 00:46:11,080
that you can't distinguish from each other,

143
00:46:11,080 --> 00:46:14,360
and then play strategically from there.

144
00:46:14,360 --> 00:46:17,680
So simultaneous moves are equivalent to sequential moves

145
00:46:17,680 --> 00:46:19,640
with uncertainty, which means that we

146
00:46:19,640 --> 00:46:22,280
can keep the nice tree format even if people

147
00:46:22,280 --> 00:46:23,480
are moving at the same time.

148
00:46:23,480 --> 00:46:25,200
Because if you, because otherwise it

149
00:46:25,200 --> 00:46:26,960
would be hard to understand what do you do if people

150
00:46:26,960 --> 00:46:27,880
move at the same time.

151
00:46:27,880 --> 00:46:29,440
How can you show that in a tree?

152
00:46:29,440 --> 00:46:33,880
The answer is we just treat that as if everything is sequential,

153
00:46:33,880 --> 00:46:36,680
but sometimes you don't know what's happening.

154
00:46:36,680 --> 00:46:39,120
And then we sort of spray paint some of the nodes

155
00:46:39,120 --> 00:46:41,760
to show that you don't know where you are in the tree,

156
00:46:41,760 --> 00:46:43,720
but it still looks like a tree.

157
00:46:43,720 --> 00:46:47,560
You just don't know where in the tree you are.

158
00:46:47,560 --> 00:46:49,920
All right, so extensive forms are done occasionally,

159
00:46:49,920 --> 00:46:51,640
especially for simple games.

160
00:46:51,640 --> 00:46:55,320
But it is more common for economists

161
00:46:55,320 --> 00:46:58,520
to use what are called moral forms.

162
00:46:58,520 --> 00:47:00,840
Someone write on that?

163
00:47:00,840 --> 00:47:02,080
Yes, someone write on that?

164
00:47:02,080 --> 00:47:05,040
Oh, that is, yes.

165
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:06,000
All right.

166
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:07,320
I didn't do it.

