255
00:51:06,480 --> 00:51:08,400
And if he plays delta, the game ends.

256
00:51:08,400 --> 00:51:10,880
And if he plays alpha, player one chooses little a or little

257
00:51:10,880 --> 00:51:11,360
d.

258
00:51:11,360 --> 00:51:12,920
And then either way, the game ends.

259
00:51:12,920 --> 00:51:15,680
And then we can write down the normal form.

260
00:51:15,680 --> 00:51:17,800
And notice this normal form is going

261
00:51:17,800 --> 00:51:21,440
to do what I mentioned, which is include information

262
00:51:21,440 --> 00:51:24,320
about irrelevant possibilities.

263
00:51:24,320 --> 00:51:26,920
So here we've got all the possible combinations

264
00:51:26,920 --> 00:51:30,480
of capital A, little a, capital A, little d, capital D,

265
00:51:30,480 --> 00:51:33,480
little a, capital D, little d.

266
00:51:33,480 --> 00:51:36,040
And those are all player one's options.

267
00:51:36,040 --> 00:51:39,920
And then here we've got alpha and delta.

268
00:51:39,920 --> 00:51:42,600
And here we have 3, 1, 4, 3.

269
00:51:42,600 --> 00:51:49,160
2, 3, 4, 3, 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, and 1, 2.

270
00:51:49,160 --> 00:51:50,160
All right.

271
00:51:50,160 --> 00:51:54,480
So in terms of common sense, you might say, well, look.

272
00:51:54,480 --> 00:51:56,840
If he plays capital D, the game ends.

273
00:51:56,840 --> 00:51:59,560
So what does it mean to say that he would have done a or b

274
00:51:59,560 --> 00:52:02,560
or a, little a or little d in a situation where he never

275
00:52:02,560 --> 00:52:04,960
does those things?

276
00:52:04,960 --> 00:52:07,960
And that is an important question, which we'll come to.

277
00:52:07,960 --> 00:52:09,040
But never mind.

278
00:52:09,040 --> 00:52:12,680
And that is an important question, which we'll come to.

279
00:52:12,680 --> 00:52:15,640
But notice that the normal form includes that information.

280
00:52:15,640 --> 00:52:23,320
So every possible scenario, so what's the right way?

281
00:52:23,320 --> 00:52:25,760
Even things that could never happen given earlier choice,

282
00:52:25,760 --> 00:52:30,200
still we go and write them down in the grid for completeness.

283
00:52:30,200 --> 00:52:33,840
Now, is this purely a mathematical fetishism thing

284
00:52:33,840 --> 00:52:35,720
where we go in, which you wouldn't put it past

285
00:52:35,720 --> 00:52:37,320
mathematicians, just saying.

286
00:52:37,320 --> 00:52:39,640
We need to cover all our basics, including the basics that

287
00:52:39,640 --> 00:52:42,680
cannot be occupied.

288
00:52:42,680 --> 00:52:44,520
But no, actually, it turns out there's

289
00:52:44,520 --> 00:52:47,760
some economic intuition to doing this, which I think

290
00:52:47,760 --> 00:52:51,920
we'll be able to get to around the end of today.

291
00:52:51,920 --> 00:52:53,080
All right.

292
00:52:53,080 --> 00:52:55,080
All right, so you can look at the situation now.

293
00:52:55,080 --> 00:52:58,600
From this, can you write down the sense of form?

294
00:52:58,600 --> 00:53:00,720
And of course, the answer is yes.

295
00:53:00,720 --> 00:53:02,520
All right, so as I said, so player one

296
00:53:02,520 --> 00:53:06,600
chooses a, capital A or capital D. And he plays D,

297
00:53:06,600 --> 00:53:07,720
the game ends.

298
00:53:07,720 --> 00:53:09,080
Now, based upon this normal form,

299
00:53:09,080 --> 00:53:13,840
what are the payoffs from over there?

300
00:53:13,840 --> 00:53:15,760
What happens?

301
00:53:15,760 --> 00:53:17,880
What always happens when capital D gets played?

302
00:53:17,880 --> 00:53:18,560
1, 2.

303
00:53:18,560 --> 00:53:20,040
1, 2, yes.

304
00:53:20,040 --> 00:53:21,400
So 1, 2.

305
00:53:21,400 --> 00:53:23,120
All right, and then if you play capital A,

306
00:53:23,120 --> 00:53:26,400
then there's alpha or delta possibilities.

307
00:53:26,400 --> 00:53:29,120
And again, if you play delta, the game ends.

308
00:53:29,120 --> 00:53:30,960
So what happens then?

309
00:53:30,960 --> 00:53:35,040
So what are the payoffs if the first person plays A,

310
00:53:35,040 --> 00:53:37,000
second person plays delta?

311
00:53:37,000 --> 00:53:37,680
4, 3.

312
00:53:37,680 --> 00:53:39,880
So we got that.

313
00:53:39,880 --> 00:53:41,920
And then finally, for this, then there's

314
00:53:41,920 --> 00:53:44,280
capital A or little a, little d.

315
00:53:44,280 --> 00:53:48,240
And then for those, we've got, so A, alpha, A.

316
00:53:48,240 --> 00:53:52,080
So that's 3, 1, and 2, 3.

317
00:53:52,080 --> 00:53:56,320
So that is the sense of form of that normal form.

318
00:53:56,320 --> 00:53:57,320
OK.

319
00:53:57,320 --> 00:53:59,000
All right, questions?

320
00:53:59,000 --> 00:53:59,500
Yes?

321
00:53:59,500 --> 00:54:02,920
So how do we know if the players play simultaneously?

322
00:54:02,920 --> 00:54:05,560
So the answer is, if we don't actually

323
00:54:05,560 --> 00:54:10,480
put those dotted lines around two or more of the nodes,

324
00:54:10,480 --> 00:54:12,240
they play sequentially.

325
00:54:12,240 --> 00:54:16,200
Is what we're seeing here.

326
00:54:18,720 --> 00:54:20,920
So in other words, if you didn't know where you were,

327
00:54:20,920 --> 00:54:25,960
then we'd have to go and put dotted lines here around them.

328
00:54:25,960 --> 00:54:27,840
OK, all right.

329
00:54:27,840 --> 00:54:30,240
Now, all that we've done so far is

330
00:54:30,240 --> 00:54:34,000
we've learned some vocabulary, some notation.

331
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:36,240
So none of this actually tells us anything

332
00:54:36,240 --> 00:54:38,200
about the real world so far.

333
00:54:38,200 --> 00:54:44,120
Now we get to the first answer to a really big question that

334
00:54:44,120 --> 00:54:46,280
has many answers.

335
00:54:46,280 --> 00:54:48,400
There are many answers to this question.

336
00:54:48,400 --> 00:54:50,200
But the first answer to the question,

337
00:54:50,200 --> 00:54:54,160
so what does game theory say that people actually do?

338
00:54:54,160 --> 00:54:54,960
All right.

339
00:54:54,960 --> 00:54:56,840
Well, normally in game theory, people

340
00:54:56,840 --> 00:54:59,680
begin trying to solve a game using the weakest

341
00:54:59,680 --> 00:55:02,400
possible assumptions and see if they get an answer.

342
00:55:02,400 --> 00:55:04,560
And if they get an answer, then they say, all right,

343
00:55:04,560 --> 00:55:07,000
let's test to see whether that answer is true.

344
00:55:07,000 --> 00:55:09,440
If they can't get an answer using weak assumptions,

345
00:55:09,440 --> 00:55:11,760
then they strengthen the assumptions a bit

346
00:55:11,760 --> 00:55:13,640
and then see if they get an answer

347
00:55:13,640 --> 00:55:16,040
and then test if that assumption is true.

348
00:55:16,040 --> 00:55:18,000
And so on.

349
00:55:18,000 --> 00:55:20,560
So start with some relatively weak assumptions,

350
00:55:20,560 --> 00:55:24,640
then strengthen them until a plausible answer emerges.

351
00:55:24,640 --> 00:55:28,480
Now, the weakest assumption that game theorists use.

352
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:32,320
The weakest assumption is that nobody

353
00:55:32,320 --> 00:55:34,960
plays what are called strictly dominated strategies.

354
00:55:42,960 --> 00:55:46,240
So slash through it.

355
00:55:46,240 --> 00:55:48,080
Nobody does those.

356
00:55:48,080 --> 00:55:51,000
All right, that is the first and weakest assumption

357
00:55:51,000 --> 00:55:54,840
that game theorists use to try to solve models in game theory.

358
00:55:54,840 --> 00:55:57,480
So what is a strictly dominated strategy?

359
00:55:57,480 --> 00:55:59,040
A strictly dominated strategy is one

360
00:55:59,040 --> 00:56:02,760
that is worse for you in all conditions of the world.

361
00:56:02,760 --> 00:56:05,160
No matter what your opponents do,

362
00:56:05,160 --> 00:56:07,240
if a strategy is worse for you than another one,

363
00:56:07,240 --> 00:56:09,280
that strategy is strictly dominated.

364
00:56:09,280 --> 00:56:11,040
And the assumption is that people

365
00:56:11,040 --> 00:56:13,720
don't play that strategy.

366
00:56:13,720 --> 00:56:15,680
All right, that sounds really reasonable, right?

367
00:56:15,680 --> 00:56:17,640
If there's something that is always a bad idea,

368
00:56:17,640 --> 00:56:20,360
no one will ever do it.

369
00:56:20,360 --> 00:56:25,160
So of course, if you know any psychology or psychology

370
00:56:25,160 --> 00:56:29,120
economics, you will know some counter examples.

371
00:56:29,120 --> 00:56:32,160
But people do do things that are bad for them no matter what.

372
00:56:32,160 --> 00:56:36,400
So in any case, we're just starting out in a simple place.

373
00:56:36,400 --> 00:56:40,080
All right, so people don't play strictly dominated strategies.

374
00:56:40,080 --> 00:56:42,240
And so if there's a strategy that is worse for you,

375
00:56:42,240 --> 00:56:44,840
no matter what your opponent does, you don't play it.

376
00:56:44,840 --> 00:56:47,680
Now, the way you get more mileage out of this

377
00:56:47,680 --> 00:56:50,840
is that if nobody plays strictly dominated strategies,

378
00:56:50,840 --> 00:56:53,680
then sometimes once you realize, well,

379
00:56:53,680 --> 00:56:56,840
that guy will never play that strategy, then you'll say, ah.

380
00:56:56,840 --> 00:56:59,600
So then actually, I can eliminate something else

381
00:56:59,600 --> 00:57:01,680
because I know that he'll never do that thing that

382
00:57:01,680 --> 00:57:02,680
is always bad for him.

383
00:57:02,680 --> 00:57:05,520
So I can ignore it when I figure out what to do.

384
00:57:05,520 --> 00:57:08,880
And I'll act as if that possibility doesn't even exist.

385
00:57:08,880 --> 00:57:11,960
And then I will perhaps be able to eliminate some strategies,

386
00:57:11,960 --> 00:57:14,480
which might mean that he'll be able to eliminate some more.

387
00:57:14,480 --> 00:57:16,320
And then I'll be able to eliminate some more.

388
00:57:16,320 --> 00:57:19,960
And so on, it may be that we wind up with only one thing

389
00:57:19,960 --> 00:57:21,480
that we do, right?

390
00:57:21,480 --> 00:57:24,000
Even though on the surface, it looks really complicated,

391
00:57:24,000 --> 00:57:26,960
it might not actually be very complicated after a while

392
00:57:26,960 --> 00:57:29,400
when we really play through.

393
00:57:29,400 --> 00:57:30,080
OK.

394
00:57:30,080 --> 00:57:34,000
Now, if eliminating all the strictly dominated strategies

395
00:57:34,000 --> 00:57:36,120
finally leaves you with only one equilibrium,

396
00:57:36,120 --> 00:57:37,640
then we have a special name for that.

397
00:57:37,640 --> 00:57:40,160
We say that the game is dominant solvable.

398
00:57:40,160 --> 00:57:41,880
It's dominant solvable.

399
00:57:41,880 --> 00:57:44,880
The classic example, of course, is the prisoner's lemma.

400
00:57:44,880 --> 00:57:47,560
Everyone here has seen the prisoner's lemma at some point.

401
00:57:47,560 --> 00:57:49,520
So the whole idea of the prisoner's lemma,

402
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:52,400
or there's many versions, but the classic version is,

403
00:57:52,400 --> 00:57:55,080
the policeman has two suspects for a crime.

404
00:57:55,080 --> 00:57:57,320
And he tells each of them, look, you can either

405
00:57:57,320 --> 00:57:59,280
confess or not confess.

406
00:57:59,280 --> 00:58:04,120
If you confess and your partner doesn't,

407
00:58:04,120 --> 00:58:05,880
then you're going to get a light sentence.

408
00:58:05,880 --> 00:58:08,960
And your partner, rather, is going to get a heavy sentence.

409
00:58:08,960 --> 00:58:10,760
If he confesses and you don't, then you're

410
00:58:10,760 --> 00:58:11,720
going to get a heavy sentence.

411
00:58:11,720 --> 00:58:13,320
And he's going to get off lightly.

412
00:58:13,320 --> 00:58:14,920
If neither of you confesses, then I'll

413
00:58:14,920 --> 00:58:16,640
go and pin on a small crime to you.

414
00:58:16,640 --> 00:58:18,960
You'll both get a traffic ticket or something.

415
00:58:18,960 --> 00:58:22,080
But if both of you confess, then you both get a harsh penalty,

416
00:58:22,080 --> 00:58:24,320
although not quite as harsh as being

417
00:58:24,320 --> 00:58:26,400
the person that refused to testify when

418
00:58:26,400 --> 00:58:29,560
your partner did testify.

419
00:58:29,560 --> 00:58:31,800
So it's all based upon the criminal justice system.

420
00:58:31,800 --> 00:58:33,920
That's why it's called the prisoner's lemma.

421
00:58:33,920 --> 00:58:36,960
And notice where the apostrophe comes.

422
00:58:36,960 --> 00:58:39,680
Is it before the S or after the S?

423
00:58:39,680 --> 00:58:42,760
It's after to show it is a lemma that collectively belongs

424
00:58:42,760 --> 00:58:44,720
to the two prisoners.

425
00:58:44,720 --> 00:58:50,000
And the prisoners are in a difficult strategic situation.

426
00:58:50,000 --> 00:58:53,880
Right now, there's a very strong intuition

427
00:58:53,880 --> 00:58:57,160
that if the prisoners can just talk and say, don't talk, OK,

428
00:58:57,160 --> 00:59:00,560
no way I would ever talk, that this changes the game.

429
00:59:00,560 --> 00:59:04,440
But in terms of the actual payoffs and the logic of the game,

430
00:59:04,440 --> 00:59:06,040
it doesn't change it at all.

431
00:59:06,040 --> 00:59:08,720
We think that talking would change the outcome.

432
00:59:08,720 --> 00:59:10,760
By the way, is there experimental evidence that

433
00:59:10,760 --> 00:59:12,880
talking does change outcomes?

434
00:59:12,880 --> 00:59:14,200
Yeah, there is.

435
00:59:14,200 --> 00:59:17,320
But it's not because of the economics or the game theory.

436
00:59:17,320 --> 00:59:19,320
It's because of psychology.

437
00:59:19,320 --> 00:59:22,600
It's because when people talk, this changes the way they feel.

438
00:59:22,600 --> 00:59:26,480
It's not because it changes what is actually in your interest.

439
00:59:26,480 --> 00:59:29,440
Because again, in terms of the actual payoffs

440
00:59:29,440 --> 00:59:33,040
in the prisoner's lemma, after you say,

441
00:59:33,040 --> 00:59:36,080
I promise I would never betray you, so don't betray me, OK?

442
00:59:36,080 --> 00:59:37,040
OK, brother.

443
00:59:37,040 --> 00:59:39,360
And then as soon as you leave the room, say, all right,

444
00:59:39,360 --> 00:59:41,840
you totally did it.

445
00:59:41,840 --> 00:59:47,840
And that is, again, in terms of the game theory, what happens.

446
00:59:47,840 --> 00:59:50,840
So part of it's that sentimental commitment that you've made.

447
00:59:50,840 --> 00:59:54,920
I think you're also identifying the probability

448
00:59:54,920 --> 00:59:56,240
that these boxes are different.

449
00:59:56,240 --> 00:59:57,760
Well, in other words, you could also

450
00:59:57,760 --> 01:00:00,160
say you're just identifying whether the person underneath


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:02,120
understands the game, right?

2
01:00:02,120 --> 01:00:03,640
And there's actually a whole class of models

3
01:00:03,640 --> 01:00:05,320
based on the idea, well, maybe your opponent

4
01:00:05,320 --> 01:00:07,920
is just clueless, right?

5
01:00:07,920 --> 01:00:09,360
You're like, so the Nobel Prize winner,

6
01:00:09,360 --> 01:00:11,520
Reiner and Zelten, got his Nobel Prize

7
01:00:11,520 --> 01:00:13,240
for some work like this, but of course,

8
01:00:13,240 --> 01:00:15,520
doing it very mathematically, right?

9
01:00:15,520 --> 01:00:17,200
Or, you know, then there's the other possibility,

10
01:00:17,200 --> 01:00:18,960
yes, maybe it turns out that you don't understand

11
01:00:18,960 --> 01:00:21,200
the payoffs, so maybe actually you learn,

12
01:00:21,200 --> 01:00:22,360
oh, he's not the kind of person

13
01:00:22,360 --> 01:00:24,160
that would be comfortable doing that.

14
01:00:24,160 --> 01:00:25,560
Sucker, ha ha.

15
01:00:25,560 --> 01:00:27,400
All right, so, or, you know, oh, well,

16
01:00:27,400 --> 01:00:29,520
I would never betray someone like that.

17
01:00:30,600 --> 01:00:32,000
Okay.

18
01:00:32,000 --> 01:00:33,400
All right, so, you know, the classic example

19
01:00:33,400 --> 01:00:34,920
is the Prisoner's Lemma, and again,

20
01:00:34,920 --> 01:00:36,320
the logic of the Prisoner's Lemma,

21
01:00:36,320 --> 01:00:38,960
the people try to weasel out of it

22
01:00:38,960 --> 01:00:41,960
with great effort, but as long as you stay

23
01:00:41,960 --> 01:00:43,580
within the game theoretic framework,

24
01:00:43,580 --> 01:00:45,280
there's no weaseling out of it, right?

25
01:00:45,280 --> 01:00:46,640
If those really are the payoffs,

26
01:00:46,640 --> 01:00:49,920
if that's what people, if that is what everybody knows,

27
01:00:49,920 --> 01:00:53,680
and if they really, and you correctly capture

28
01:00:53,680 --> 01:00:55,920
the utility functions with those payoffs,

29
01:00:55,920 --> 01:00:58,280
then that is what they're going to do.

30
01:00:58,280 --> 01:01:00,520
All right, so that's as simple as that.

31
01:01:01,360 --> 01:01:02,680
Of course, you say, well, what if we experimentally

32
01:01:02,680 --> 01:01:03,520
tested this wrong?

33
01:01:03,520 --> 01:01:05,440
Well, there's something wrong with the assumptions,

34
01:01:05,440 --> 01:01:06,280
isn't there?

35
01:01:06,280 --> 01:01:08,880
All right, so, the model is not actually very good.

36
01:01:10,280 --> 01:01:11,880
Okay.

37
01:01:11,880 --> 01:01:13,920
All right, so anyway, so if all players think this way,

38
01:01:13,920 --> 01:01:17,080
you can extend the idea to success and strict dominance,

39
01:01:17,080 --> 01:01:19,320
which, as I said, allows you to eliminate

40
01:01:19,320 --> 01:01:20,960
even more possibilities than you might think,

41
01:01:20,960 --> 01:01:22,800
if your opponent would never play a strategy,

42
01:01:22,800 --> 01:01:26,240
you can cross out or erase that row or column,

43
01:01:26,240 --> 01:01:29,400
which may then clean up the game, right?

44
01:01:29,400 --> 01:01:31,080
And then when you clean up the game,

45
01:01:31,080 --> 01:01:32,520
so you'll then, it may be, okay,

46
01:01:32,520 --> 01:01:35,080
now I understand what I'm going to do, right?

47
01:01:35,080 --> 01:01:36,700
Like if you've ever had a homework problem

48
01:01:36,700 --> 01:01:38,960
where you're stumped and then you say, wait a second,

49
01:01:38,960 --> 01:01:40,900
player two always plays A.

50
01:01:41,840 --> 01:01:44,680
That simplifies things a lot, doesn't it?

51
01:01:44,680 --> 01:01:47,480
Right, so, or again, you can claim,

52
01:01:47,480 --> 01:01:48,680
you have lots of ways to clean up a game,

53
01:01:48,680 --> 01:01:50,080
saying, oh, well, perfect competition,

54
01:01:50,080 --> 01:01:52,260
so they always set prices with a marginal cost,

55
01:01:52,260 --> 01:01:54,600
so we don't need to worry about what they are doing.

56
01:01:54,600 --> 01:01:57,480
We know, we now know what they are doing all the time.

57
01:01:58,640 --> 01:02:01,200
Okay, all right, now a fun fact,

58
01:02:01,200 --> 01:02:02,800
which I will state but not prove,

59
01:02:02,800 --> 01:02:05,960
is that the order of iteration does not matter.

60
01:02:05,960 --> 01:02:08,960
So there's no danger when you're doing strict dominance

61
01:02:08,960 --> 01:02:11,800
that you might go and eliminate something

62
01:02:11,800 --> 01:02:14,120
before something else, and that will change the outcome.

63
01:02:14,120 --> 01:02:16,560
However, whichever way, whichever route you proceed,

