88
01:23:37,800 --> 01:23:40,000
wants to end the game two turns early.

89
01:23:40,000 --> 01:23:41,200
But if I know that he's gonna do that,

90
01:23:41,200 --> 01:23:42,760
I want to end it three turns early.

91
01:23:42,760 --> 01:23:45,680
If he knows I want to end it four turns early and so on.

92
01:23:45,680 --> 01:23:47,920
Right, so if we go and go and start solving this

93
01:23:47,920 --> 01:23:51,000
by backwards induction, in that second to last turn,

94
01:23:51,000 --> 01:23:53,720
the second player can either have 100 or 101.

95
01:23:53,720 --> 01:23:55,680
So what's he gonna do?

96
01:23:55,680 --> 01:23:56,600
Get 101.

97
01:23:56,600 --> 01:23:59,280
So we can go and erase that last possibility

98
01:23:59,280 --> 01:24:00,840
that'll never happen.

99
01:24:00,840 --> 01:24:03,360
Right, and now the first player will say,

100
01:24:03,360 --> 01:24:05,680
well I can either go and have 99 or 98.

101
01:24:05,680 --> 01:24:07,320
Which one do I prefer?

102
01:24:07,320 --> 01:24:12,320
99, so I'll go and erase that part of the centipede.

103
01:24:12,320 --> 01:24:13,680
And then he'll go and erase it,

104
01:24:13,680 --> 01:24:15,360
and erase it, and erase it, and erase it.

105
01:24:15,360 --> 01:24:18,520
And we erase all the way back to turn one.

106
01:24:18,520 --> 01:24:21,080
And then in turn one, the other player just says,

107
01:24:21,080 --> 01:24:23,320
well I see where this is going.

108
01:24:23,320 --> 01:24:25,800
I refuse to play this game at all.

109
01:24:25,800 --> 01:24:27,160
I'm just gonna get one.

110
01:24:27,160 --> 01:24:29,000
Because otherwise I'm gonna get zero.

111
01:24:30,120 --> 01:24:32,160
Right, so you notice if the game ends in turn one,

112
01:24:32,160 --> 01:24:33,000
player one gets one.

113
01:24:33,000 --> 01:24:36,040
But if it ends in turns two, player one gets zero.

114
01:24:36,040 --> 01:24:39,760
All right, so here is a use of backwards induction,

115
01:24:39,760 --> 01:24:42,840
which yields through an external force of logic

116
01:24:42,840 --> 01:24:44,160
a very surprising result.

117
01:24:44,160 --> 01:24:47,840
Namely that even though it seems like total common sense

118
01:24:47,840 --> 01:24:49,560
for the two players to work together,

119
01:24:49,560 --> 01:24:51,320
they will not work together at all.

120
01:24:52,360 --> 01:24:55,560
There's a variant of common sense which says,

121
01:24:55,560 --> 01:24:58,560
fine, cooperation will break down towards the end sometime.

122
01:25:00,760 --> 01:25:03,200
But backwards induction doesn't say

123
01:25:03,200 --> 01:25:05,440
it'll break down towards the end sometime.

124
01:25:05,440 --> 01:25:07,640
It says it'll break down immediately.

125
01:25:07,640 --> 01:25:10,120
Because people say, what, towards the end sometime?

126
01:25:10,120 --> 01:25:11,280
That's not a time.

127
01:25:12,400 --> 01:25:13,240
No, no, no.

128
01:25:13,240 --> 01:25:16,640
So it'll definitely have broken down by the last turn.

129
01:25:16,640 --> 01:25:18,920
So there's no point cooperating the second to last turn.

130
01:25:18,920 --> 01:25:20,640
So no point cooperating in the third to last turn.

131
01:25:20,640 --> 01:25:22,800
So no cooperating in the fourth to last turn.

132
01:25:22,800 --> 01:25:24,200
All the way back.

133
01:25:24,200 --> 01:25:28,000
And the game does what we sometimes call unravel.

134
01:25:28,000 --> 01:25:29,520
Game theorists say unraveling.

135
01:25:29,520 --> 01:25:31,720
If you ever were a kid and you had a string on your clothes

136
01:25:31,720 --> 01:25:34,960
and you started pulling and pulling and pulling and pulling

137
01:25:34,960 --> 01:25:36,680
and eventually you had no clothes left,

138
01:25:36,680 --> 01:25:37,840
just a pile of thread.

139
01:25:38,760 --> 01:25:40,280
I don't think I would have caught that far,

140
01:25:40,280 --> 01:25:42,840
but I definitely did ruin a shirt or two.

141
01:25:42,840 --> 01:25:44,160
I was laying on my end.

142
01:25:44,160 --> 01:25:46,120
And I'm like, oh, it's not my fault.

143
01:25:46,120 --> 01:25:47,360
It just happened.

144
01:25:48,360 --> 01:25:49,960
Didn't happen, you pulled it.

145
01:25:49,960 --> 01:25:52,000
Why didn't you leave it alone?

146
01:25:52,000 --> 01:25:53,560
I don't like leaving things alone.

147
01:25:53,560 --> 01:25:55,400
I try to fix them.

148
01:25:55,400 --> 01:25:57,440
You see, it's clearly getting worse.

149
01:26:00,080 --> 01:26:02,120
There's also the great example of the mosquito bite.

150
01:26:02,120 --> 01:26:03,200
How many times have you been told,

151
01:26:03,200 --> 01:26:05,960
don't scratch a mosquito bite, it will make it worse?

152
01:26:05,960 --> 01:26:09,120
In my experience, this is true always.

153
01:26:09,120 --> 01:26:12,880
And yet, how many people scratch mosquito bites?

154
01:26:12,880 --> 01:26:13,960
So tempting.

155
01:26:13,960 --> 01:26:16,920
It's like, I can't just sit here suffering, doing nothing.

156
01:26:16,920 --> 01:26:19,760
I've got to do something that makes it worse.

157
01:26:19,760 --> 01:26:21,560
And I believe this is a metaphor

158
01:26:21,560 --> 01:26:23,160
economists really need to push.

159
01:26:25,080 --> 01:26:27,080
Look, we all know about mosquito bites

160
01:26:27,080 --> 01:26:28,480
and how we make problems worse

161
01:26:28,480 --> 01:26:30,920
by trying to solve them, right?

162
01:26:30,920 --> 01:26:32,880
That one's undeniable.

163
01:26:32,880 --> 01:26:35,200
Maybe there's other things like that in the world.

164
01:26:35,200 --> 01:26:36,880
Maybe there's economic problems,

165
01:26:36,880 --> 01:26:39,800
which we amplify by trying to solve them.

166
01:26:39,800 --> 01:26:42,080
And if someone says, but I can't live with this problem,

167
01:26:42,080 --> 01:26:44,120
I say, well, can you live with a worse problem?

168
01:26:44,120 --> 01:26:46,880
Because that's all you're going to do, is make it worse.

169
01:26:46,880 --> 01:26:49,400
But anyway, that's the centipede game.

170
01:26:49,400 --> 01:26:51,040
Now, as you might guess, in experiments,

171
01:26:51,040 --> 01:26:54,480
do people actually go and immediately stop cooperating

172
01:26:54,480 --> 01:26:56,200
and turn the light on in the centipede game?

173
01:26:56,200 --> 01:26:57,400
They do not.

174
01:26:57,400 --> 01:26:59,480
All right, and in fact, that common sense prediction

175
01:26:59,480 --> 01:27:01,080
of people, cooperation will break down

176
01:27:01,080 --> 01:27:03,000
somewhere near the end.

177
01:27:03,000 --> 01:27:04,800
That actually is pretty right.

178
01:27:04,800 --> 01:27:08,840
So like somewhere between turns 95 and 98,

179
01:27:08,840 --> 01:27:11,840
that's about when people stop cooperating.

180
01:27:11,840 --> 01:27:13,760
And as to when exactly that is varies,

181
01:27:13,760 --> 01:27:14,960
and of course, there are some people who do it

182
01:27:14,960 --> 01:27:16,960
to the very bitter end,

183
01:27:16,960 --> 01:27:19,320
but like sometime in the 90s

184
01:27:19,320 --> 01:27:21,800
is where actual cooperation normally breaks down.

185
01:27:22,720 --> 01:27:25,920
So here, this vague intuition about,

186
01:27:25,920 --> 01:27:27,040
of course, they'll cooperate,

187
01:27:27,040 --> 01:27:30,320
but it will kind of break down as the end approaches,

188
01:27:30,320 --> 01:27:33,000
that turns out to be more true, certainly,

189
01:27:33,000 --> 01:27:35,280
then there will be no cooperation.

190
01:27:35,280 --> 01:27:38,120
But in any case, the concept backwards deduction

191
01:27:38,120 --> 01:27:40,760
does logically imply that there will be no cooperation

192
01:27:40,760 --> 01:27:41,840
at all in this game.

193
01:27:45,240 --> 01:27:49,080
All right, so and again, why is that?

194
01:27:49,080 --> 01:27:51,080
Well, we're going to talk about some models

195
01:27:51,080 --> 01:27:53,440
that might explain real world behavior.

196
01:27:53,440 --> 01:27:55,920
Although, again, the longer I stay in economics,

197
01:27:55,920 --> 01:27:58,640
the more I am partial to asking,

198
01:27:58,640 --> 01:28:00,040
well, what would psychologists say about this?

199
01:28:00,040 --> 01:28:01,680
Isn't that clearly true?

200
01:28:01,680 --> 01:28:04,160
And then we could go and try to model that,

201
01:28:04,160 --> 01:28:06,280
or we could just say that they actually

202
01:28:06,280 --> 01:28:08,400
have more predictive science in this case.

203
01:28:09,840 --> 01:28:11,560
Could it be that psychologists know more than us

204
01:28:11,560 --> 01:28:12,920
on a bunch of things?

205
01:28:12,920 --> 01:28:13,960
I don't know why not.

206
01:28:16,680 --> 01:28:17,920
So when I was in your shoes,

207
01:28:17,920 --> 01:28:20,120
like the word psychology can never be said

208
01:28:20,120 --> 01:28:22,480
without sarcasm, italics.

209
01:28:22,480 --> 01:28:25,720
Well, you just want to be a psychologist about this.

210
01:28:25,720 --> 01:28:30,160
So you can never even say the word without hostility,

211
01:28:30,160 --> 01:28:33,400
but we do need to learn to use the word without hostility

212
01:28:33,400 --> 01:28:36,360
because it has so much to teach us.

213
01:28:36,360 --> 01:28:38,280
Whenever you're cackling with glee

214
01:28:38,280 --> 01:28:41,720
about some psychology experiment failed to replicate,

215
01:28:41,720 --> 01:28:44,520
there's a lot of other stuff that does replicate.

216
01:28:44,520 --> 01:28:46,400
So of course, there's a lot of economic theories

217
01:28:46,400 --> 01:28:48,520
that haven't even been shown to work once.

218
01:28:48,520 --> 01:28:51,880
So I don't know if the CentiB game

219
01:28:51,880 --> 01:28:54,200
has ever been confirmed at all,

220
01:28:54,200 --> 01:28:55,560
like the backwards induction version

221
01:28:55,560 --> 01:28:56,840
where 100, 100 turn game,

222
01:28:56,840 --> 01:28:58,840
cooperation breaks down immediately.

223
01:28:58,840 --> 01:29:00,440
You'd have to really think about how you would

224
01:29:00,440 --> 01:29:01,720
make it break down.

225
01:29:01,720 --> 01:29:03,920
Like maybe if you had it played by couples

226
01:29:03,920 --> 01:29:05,320
in the middle of a divorce,

227
01:29:05,320 --> 01:29:08,920
then maybe they would play this way.

228
01:29:08,920 --> 01:29:11,160
Maybe, even there I don't know.

229
01:29:12,760 --> 01:29:16,640
Okay, so questions before we move on?

230
01:29:18,680 --> 01:29:20,000
All right, so now let's move on

231
01:29:20,000 --> 01:29:23,280
to pure strategy Nash equilibrium, right?

232
01:29:23,280 --> 01:29:25,480
And this is a concept that is only a much more

233
01:29:25,480 --> 01:29:29,160
practically useful than the ones we just done.

234
01:29:29,160 --> 01:29:31,400
So you can only go so far

235
01:29:31,400 --> 01:29:33,360
with strict dominance type reasoning.

236
01:29:33,360 --> 01:29:35,000
Backwards induction seems really awesome

237
01:29:35,000 --> 01:29:36,160
when you first hear it,

238
01:29:36,160 --> 01:29:37,760
until you realize it only works

239
01:29:37,760 --> 01:29:40,360
for these games of perfect information anyway.

240
01:29:40,360 --> 01:29:43,200
All right, so and how many interesting situations

241
01:29:43,200 --> 01:29:44,880
have involved perfect information?

242
01:29:46,600 --> 01:29:47,920
I've never found one.

243
01:29:47,920 --> 01:29:50,000
All right, so I ran into that little friend

244
01:29:50,000 --> 01:29:50,840
who says that something,

245
01:29:50,840 --> 01:29:52,760
let's see, I guess they're on Facebook.

246
01:29:52,760 --> 01:29:54,120
There's like, what are the odds

247
01:29:54,120 --> 01:29:56,920
that Roe versus Wade gets repealed under Trump?

248
01:29:56,920 --> 01:29:59,320
And then a bunch of people say zero.

249
01:29:59,320 --> 01:30:00,160
Zero?

250
01:30:00,160 --> 01:30:03,200
How could it be zero?

251
01:30:03,200 --> 01:30:04,040
Come on.

252
01:30:05,760 --> 01:30:06,600
That's crazy.

253
01:30:06,600 --> 01:30:07,440
How could it be?

254
01:30:07,440 --> 01:30:10,200
Like I said, 15%, that's the answer.

255
01:30:10,200 --> 01:30:12,040
All right, now my answer may be wrong,

256
01:30:12,040 --> 01:30:14,720
but I know that's a way better answer than zero.

257
01:30:16,000 --> 01:30:17,320
Which is just ridiculous.

258
01:30:18,560 --> 01:30:20,200
Friend said like, there's zero chance

259
01:30:20,200 --> 01:30:22,360
of a war between Saudi Arabia and Iran.

260
01:30:22,360 --> 01:30:24,240
Zero?

261
01:30:24,240 --> 01:30:27,040
I mean, it's more likely than like the US and Canada,

262
01:30:27,040 --> 01:30:28,280
isn't it?

263
01:30:28,280 --> 01:30:31,160
So they can't both be zero.

264
01:30:31,160 --> 01:30:33,160
If one's more likely than the other.

265
01:30:34,200 --> 01:30:35,640
All right, in any case,

266
01:30:35,640 --> 01:30:37,800
so very few interesting situations

267
01:30:37,800 --> 01:30:39,880
do we actually have perfect information.

268
01:30:39,880 --> 01:30:41,520
Of course, there are some people think chess

269
01:30:41,520 --> 01:30:42,760
is the most interesting thing in the world,

270
01:30:42,760 --> 01:30:45,280
in which case, fine, all right, chess, that's it.

271
01:30:45,280 --> 01:30:47,480
Chess to me is a very boring game.

272
01:30:47,480 --> 01:30:49,520
I like games where people are laughing when they play

273
01:30:49,520 --> 01:30:51,400
and I don't see anyone laughing playing chess.

274
01:30:51,400 --> 01:30:55,840
So, if you aren't, if it isn't funny, it's not worth playing.

275
01:30:55,840 --> 01:30:56,680
That's my view.

276
01:30:57,840 --> 01:30:59,720
All right, in any case,

277
01:30:59,720 --> 01:31:02,920
this leads us to a very different equilibrium concept

278
01:31:02,920 --> 01:31:05,560
that comes from me with a totally different intuition

279
01:31:05,560 --> 01:31:07,200
than strictly dominant,

280
01:31:07,200 --> 01:31:09,840
than the strict dominance or weak dominance.

281
01:31:09,840 --> 01:31:11,760
And that is the pure strategy Nash equilibrium,

282
01:31:11,760 --> 01:31:15,000
which of course is a comes from John Nash.

283
01:31:15,000 --> 01:31:17,320
How many of you see us all beautiful mind?

284
01:31:17,320 --> 01:31:20,160
All right, so okay, movie, the book is way better.

285
01:31:20,160 --> 01:31:22,880
And yes, I have an article brawling heavily on the book

286
01:31:22,880 --> 01:31:26,600
because while the movie shows Nash being subject

287
01:31:26,600 --> 01:31:28,920
to a disease totally outside of control,

288
01:31:28,920 --> 01:31:33,360
the book actually, Nash's own story, how did I get better?

289
01:31:33,360 --> 01:31:36,640
I just decided to stop talking about nonsense.

290
01:31:36,640 --> 01:31:37,840
That's how I got better.

291
01:31:38,960 --> 01:31:41,400
All right, well, that's what he said.

292
01:31:41,400 --> 01:31:43,200
It's also part of where son with the same problems

293
01:31:43,200 --> 01:31:45,040
has said, look, mom takes care of dad

294
01:31:45,040 --> 01:31:46,000
and doesn't make him do anything.

295
01:31:46,000 --> 01:31:47,800
Why can't she give me the same deal?

296
01:31:47,800 --> 01:31:50,920
All right, sounds like there's a lot of self-awareness there

297
01:31:50,920 --> 01:31:52,840
which is not reflected in the movie.

298
01:31:52,840 --> 01:31:57,480
Anyway, so John Nash and to then after everything else

299
01:31:57,480 --> 01:32:00,440
died in a taxi a couple of years ago.

300
01:32:00,440 --> 01:32:03,000
So like a weird ending to the two,

301
01:32:03,000 --> 01:32:05,240
which of course is years after the movie.

302
01:32:05,240 --> 01:32:08,920
Any case, so named after John Nash, right?

303
01:32:08,920 --> 01:32:11,000
So instead of player strategies

304
01:32:11,000 --> 01:32:16,000
is a pure strategy Nash equilibrium abbreviated PSNE.

305
01:32:16,000 --> 01:32:19,640
This is one of the acronyms you must learn for this class.

306
01:32:19,640 --> 01:32:21,000
All right, not many, but this is one of them.

307
01:32:21,000 --> 01:32:24,440
PSNE, pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

308
01:32:24,440 --> 01:32:27,120
All right, so if you set up player strategies,

309
01:32:27,120 --> 01:32:28,920
a pure strategy Nash equilibrium,

310
01:32:28,920 --> 01:32:30,960
if no player could do strictly better

311
01:32:30,960 --> 01:32:32,440
by changing strategies,

312
01:32:32,440 --> 01:32:35,200
holding all other player strategies fixed.

313
01:32:36,360 --> 01:32:39,080
Okay, so what does that mean?

314
01:32:39,080 --> 01:32:42,160
Well, imagine asking all the players one by one

315
01:32:43,000 --> 01:32:45,240
if they would like to do something different.

316
01:32:45,240 --> 01:32:47,680
If all of them answer no,

317
01:32:47,680 --> 01:32:49,600
then you have a pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

318
01:32:49,600 --> 01:32:50,760
Like what do you mean do something different?

319
01:32:50,760 --> 01:32:53,640
Do something different given what everybody else is doing.

320
01:32:53,640 --> 01:32:55,320
So for example, here's how we test

321
01:32:55,320 --> 01:32:57,240
for pure strategy Nash equilibrium

322
01:32:57,240 --> 01:32:59,440
in the seating arrangement of this room.

323
01:32:59,440 --> 01:33:01,440
So I go to you, given where everyone else is seating,

324
01:33:01,440 --> 01:33:02,920
sitting, do you want to change your seat?

325
01:33:02,920 --> 01:33:03,760
No.

326
01:33:03,760 --> 01:33:04,960
No, given where everyone else is sitting,

327
01:33:04,960 --> 01:33:06,800
including the first two, do you want to change your seat?

328
01:33:06,800 --> 01:33:08,320
No, given where everyone else is sitting,

329
01:33:08,320 --> 01:33:09,600
including the first two people at your desk,

330
01:33:09,600 --> 01:33:10,440
do you want to change your seat?

331
01:33:10,440 --> 01:33:11,280
No.

332
01:33:11,280 --> 01:33:12,760
I go through the entire room.

333
01:33:12,760 --> 01:33:14,280
If 100% of the people say no,

334
01:33:14,280 --> 01:33:15,360
I do not want to change my seat,

335
01:33:15,360 --> 01:33:17,160
given where everybody else is,

336
01:33:17,160 --> 01:33:19,400
we've got pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

337
01:33:19,400 --> 01:33:22,320
If one student says yes, I would.

338
01:33:22,320 --> 01:33:25,360
We don't have a pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

339
01:33:25,360 --> 01:33:26,880
But again, it's not that I would like

340
01:33:26,880 --> 01:33:28,240
to trade seats with that student

341
01:33:28,240 --> 01:33:29,280
because that student is in the seat.

342
01:33:29,280 --> 01:33:30,480
It would have to be like,

343
01:33:30,480 --> 01:33:33,840
you don't want to go and evict them from the seat.

344
01:33:33,840 --> 01:33:36,800
It's got to be that given what they're doing,

345
01:33:36,800 --> 01:33:38,440
you actually want to do something differently,

346
01:33:38,440 --> 01:33:39,280
which could be saying,

347
01:33:39,280 --> 01:33:41,880
would you mind changing seats with me, sir?

348
01:33:41,880 --> 01:33:44,240
Which if you have back trouble, you should try this.

349
01:33:44,240 --> 01:33:48,840
In general, I found that when you plead a medical excuse,

350
01:33:48,840 --> 01:33:50,360
people usually switch seats with you.

351
01:33:50,360 --> 01:33:52,520
So there's no need to be suffering

352
01:33:52,520 --> 01:33:54,720
if you see a better chair around.

